---

## How the Frontend Handles SSE Events

### 1. `thinking` vs `answer` events in `agentService.ts`

Both events are treated **identically** at the SSE parsing layer — they both call `onTextChunk`:

```typescript
// agentService.ts, lines 181-184
case 'thinking':
case 'answer':
  callbacks.onTextChunk(data.text as string);
  break;
case 'thinking_done':
  callbacks.onThinkingDone();
  break;
```

The differentiation happens **above** this layer. The `AgentContext.tsx` tracks a `phaseRef` that starts as `'thinking'` and switches to `'answer'` only when `onThinkingDone` fires:

```typescript
// AgentContext.tsx, lines 33, 100-119
const phaseRef = useRef<'thinking' | 'answer'>('thinking');

onThinkingDone: () => {
  // ...flush buffer...
  if (currentTextRef.current.trim()) {
    segmentsRef.current.push({ type: 'thinking', text: currentTextRef.current });
    currentTextRef.current = '';
  }
  phaseRef.current = 'answer';   // <-- phase flips here
  setMessages((prev) =>
    prev.map((m) =>
      m.id === assistantId
        ? { ...m, currentPhase: 'answer', segments: [...segmentsRef.current] }
        : m
    )
  );
},
```

On `onDone`, any remaining accumulated text is flushed as an `'answer'` segment regardless of the current phase:

```typescript
// AgentContext.tsx, lines 223-228
if (currentTextRef.current.trim()) {
  segmentsRef.current.push({
    type: 'answer',
    text: currentTextRef.current,
  });
```

So: if `thinking_done` never fires (e.g. extended thinking is not used), all text chunks accumulate and are labeled `'answer'` at the end.

---

### 2. Where the visible response text is rendered

`MessageBubble.tsx` splits segments into two groups:

**Answer segments** — rendered in a visible, labeled "Answer" block:

```typescript
// MessageBubble.tsx, lines 179-187
const answerSegments = hasSegments
  ? message.segments!
      .filter((s) =>
        (s.type === 'answer' && s.text?.trim()) ||
        (s.type === 'subagent_end' && !s.chart_spec && s.text?.trim())
      )
      ...
  : [];
```

```tsx
// MessageBubble.tsx, lines 289-306
{(chartSegments.length > 0 || answerSegments.length > 0) && (
  <div className="message-bubble__segment message-bubble__segment--answer">
    <div className="message-bubble__segment-label message-bubble__segment-label--answer">{t('answer')}</div>
    ...
    {answerSegments.map((seg, i) => (
      <div key={`answer-${i}`} className="message-bubble__segment-content">
        <ReactMarkdown remarkPlugins={[remarkGfm]}>{seg.text!}</ReactMarkdown>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
)}
```

---

### 3. Does thinking text get shown to the user?

**Yes — but it is collapsed inside a `<details>` disclosure widget**, not hidden entirely. The `ThinkingBlock` component wraps all non-answer segments in a `<details>` element:

```tsx
// MessageBubble.tsx, lines 40-103
function ThinkingBlock({ segments, streamingRemainder, isThinkingPhase, isAgentStreaming }) {
  // All non-answer, non-chart, non-user_question segments go inside the thinking block
  const thinkingSegments = segments.filter(
    (s) => s.type !== 'answer' && s.type !== 'user_question' && !(s.type === 'tool' && s.toolResult?.chart_spec) && !(s.type === 'subagent_end' && (s.chart_spec || s.text?.trim()))
  );
  ...
  return (
    <details
      className="message-bubble__segment message-bubble__segment--thinking message-bubble__collapsible"
      open={isThinkingPhase || undefined}  // open while agent is still thinking
    >
      <summary className="message-bubble__collapsible-summary">
        <span className="message-bubble__segment-label">{t('thinkingLabel')}</span>
        {summary && <span className="message-bubble__collapsible-preview">{summary}</span>}
      </summary>
      <div className="message-bubble__thinking-body">
        {thinkingSegments.map((seg, i) => {
          if (seg.type === 'thinking' && seg.text?.trim()) {
            return (
              <div key={i} className="message-bubble__segment-content">
                <ReactMarkdown remarkPlugins={[remarkGfm]}>{seg.text}</ReactMarkdown>
              </div>
            );
          }
          ...
        })}
      </div>
    </details>
  );
}
```

Key behavior details:

- While the agent is **still streaming in the thinking phase**, the `<details>` element is rendered with `open` (expanded), so the user sees the thinking text live.
- Once the `thinking_done` event fires and the answer phase begins, `isThinkingPhase` becomes `false`, so the `open` attribute is removed and the `<details>` **collapses**.
- After collapse, a one-line preview (the last line of thinking text, truncated to 100 characters) is shown in the `<summary>` alongside the "Thinking" label.
- The user can manually re-expand it at any time by clicking the `<details>` toggle.

```typescript
// MessageBubble.tsx, lines 172-173
const isInAnswerPhase = message.currentPhase === 'answer';
const isThinkingPhase = !!message.isStreaming && !hasAnswer && !isInAnswerPhase;
```

```typescript
// MessageBubble.tsx, lines 22-38 — preview truncation
function getLastThinkingLine(...): string {
  // ...
  return last.length > 100 ? last.slice(0, 100) + '...' : last;
}
```

---

### Summary

| Event | What happens |
|---|---|
| `thinking` | Text chunk appended to buffer; rendered inside the collapsible `ThinkingBlock` as a `ContentSegment` of `type: 'thinking'` |
| `thinking_done` | Buffer flushed → `thinking` segment saved; `phaseRef` flips to `'answer'`; `<details>` collapses |
| `answer` | Text chunk appended to same buffer (now in answer phase); on `done`, flushed as `type: 'answer'` segment rendered in the visible Answer block |

Thinking content **is visible to the user** — it streams live in an expanded `<details>` block during the thinking phase, then auto-collapses (but remains accessible via click) once the answer phase starts.

